 "notes": "Label column = outside diameter in mm; the other column labels = wall thickness in mm; cell values = theoretical mass in kg/m (decimal point as printed). Empty cells are empty in the source; no footnotes, no merged cells. 39 rows, the image starts directly with row 12. Anomalies of the SOURCE, transcribed verbatim and NOT corrected: (1) Row 15: from column 1.4 onward the printed values are shifted one column to the right - 0.408 is printed under both 1.2 and 1.4, then 0.469 under 1.5, 0.499 under 1.8, 0.586 under 2.0 and 0.641 under 2.5 (these are the weights that belong to 15x1.4, 15x1.5, 15x1.8 and 15x2.0; the weight for 15x2.5 is not printed at all). As printed, the row is not increasing left to right and is lighter than row 14 in columns 1.4, 1.8 and 2.0. (2) Row label 19.5 is printed exactly as '19.5', but all ten weights in that row are the weights of a 19.05 mm tube (3/4 inch), not of 19.5 mm. (3) Row 38 has two values printed with only two decimals ('1.35', '1.61') and values only in columns 0.8, 0.9, 1.0, 1.2, 1.5, 1.8, 2.0, 2.5. (4) Row 19 has values only in columns 0.8, 0.9, 1.0, 1.2, 1.5, 2.0. Observation for the verification step (no value was changed): apart from the cells named in (1) and with 19.05 used for (2), every printed value equals round((D-t)*t*3.141*0.00785, 3), i.e. the source apparently used pi=3.141 with density 7.85; against the exact formula (D-t)*t*pi*7850/1e6, 95 cells are therefore 0.001 kg/m lower in the last digit (never more). The two 3-decimal values of row 38 in columns 2.0 and 2.5 (1.776, 2.189) follow exact pi instead."
